# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-
R = {
'1-1-1': dict(teach=[
'Aim for one sentence the children can own: God made me because he loves me, and I was made to love him back. At six, belonging comes before ideas; they need to feel wanted first.',
'CCC 1 and 356: God freely made us to share his own blessed life, and of all visible creatures only humans can know and love their Creator. CCC 1721: we are made to know, love and serve him and come to heaven.',
'Say: “God made your heart so you could love, like a wire that lights a lamp.” If a child asks, “Why did God make me?”, answer: “Because he wanted you, and he is happiest when you are close to him.”',
'Do not tie being loved to being good, clever or obedient; some children hear that at home or school. Be careful with children who are ill, disabled or adopted: every child is wanted, no exceptions.',
'Ask each child to name one person they love and show with their hands how big God’s love is. Listen for “God made me because he loves me.” If they say “because I’m good,” gently correct it.',
]),
'1-1-2': dict(teach=[
'The one thing: God our Father knows me by name and looks after me, so I can trust him. At this age trust is the base for all later prayer, and little ones feel safe when they know someone is watching over them.',
'CCC 301: God does not leave creatures on their own; he keeps them in being at every moment. CCC 305 draws on Matthew 6: Jesus asks for childlike trust in the Father who cares for our smallest needs. CCC 2828 links this to “Give us.”',
'Say: “God knows you better than anyone, and he sends people to help: mum, dad, teacher, farmer, doctor.” If a child asks why someone is still hungry or sick, say: “God wants everyone looked after, and he asks us to help.”',
'Some children go without food, a stable home or a parent. Never imply that God gave them less. Do not say that God sends only what is needed if a child clearly lacks; stress that God asks us to share and he is with them.',
'Ask: “Who does God use to look after you?” Listen for people, not only God in the sky. A child who says “my grandma gives me dinner and God sent her” has understood.',
]),
'1-1-3': dict(teach=[
'Get across two things in order: sin breaks friendship with God, and God did not give up on us but promised a Savior. End on hope, not on guilt. Six-year-olds easily hear a story only as being told off.',
'CCC 397: the first sin was a failure of trust in the Creator and disobedience to his command. CCC 410: God called man after the fall and promised victory over evil, the first announcement of the Savior (the Protoevangelium).',
'Say: “Sin is choosing my way instead of God’s way. It makes a gap between us, and God comes looking: ‘Where are you?’” To “Why did God put the tree there?” say: “God gave a rule because he loves them; they chose not to trust him.”',
'Avoid making the serpent or the fall scary, or blaming Eve or women. Some children carry shame about something they did; stress that God wants them back. Do not teach that all pain is a punishment for a particular child’s sin.',
'Ask: “What did God do after Adam and Eve hid?” Hoped-for answers: he came looking and promised to send a Savior. If a child says he got angry and left, retell the promise.',
]),
'1-2-1': dict(teach=[
'Teach that Noah trusted God and obeyed even when it looked silly, and that God keeps his promises; the rainbow is the reminder. The point is trust and promise, not a storm.',
'CCC 56: after sin God sought to save humanity part by part, and the covenant with Noah is the first with all the nations. CCC 58: it remains in force. Genesis 9: God promises never again to flood the earth.',
'Say: “Noah did what God asked even when others laughed. God promised: ‘I will always take care of the world,’ and he put a rainbow in the sky.” If asked “Why did God make a flood?”, say: “People had stopped loving; God saved Noah’s family to start again.”',
'The flood scares some children, especially after a storm or a loss. Keep it short, show the ark as a safe place, and avoid pictures of drowning. Do not turn the story into a lesson that God punishes children with bad weather.',
'Ask the children to say what the rainbow tells us. Listen for “God keeps his promises” or “God takes care of us.” Then ask one child how Noah showed trust.',
]),
'1-2-2': dict(teach=[
'The key: Abraham trusted God and set off without knowing the way, and God kept his promise. Faith at this age means “I trust God because he is good,” not knowing everything.',
'CCC 59: God called Abram out of his home and made him Abraham, father of many nations. CCC 145: Hebrews praises Abraham for obeying by faith. The promise points to Christ and to all who believe.',
'Say: “Abraham packed up and went because God said so, like holding a friend’s hand in the dark.” To “Did Abraham miss home?”, say: “Probably, and trusting God can feel hard, but God was with him all the way.”',
'Do not treat faith as never feeling afraid. Children who move house or change school may identify with Abraham, so name that worry. Skip the sacrifice of Isaac at this age, since it can alarm children about parents.',
'Ask: “What did God ask Abraham to do? What did Abraham do?” Then: “When is it hard to trust someone?” A child who links trust to a feeling of being safe with God has got it.',
]),
'1-2-3': dict(teach=[
'The point: God first saved his people, then gave rules to show them how to stay close to him. The commandments are a gift of love, not a list of threats. Show the order: rescue, then rules.',
'CCC 2056: the Decalogue means “ten words,” revealed on the mountain. CCC 2060: the Law is part of the covenant, given after God had freed his people. The first three commandments are about loving God.',
'Say: “Fences at the playground keep us safe. God’s rules keep us safe and close to him.” To “Why do we have to?”, say: “Because God loves us and knows what makes us happy, like a parent saying look both ways.”',
'Avoid presenting God as a strict judge or the commandments as a way to earn his love. Slavery is a heavy word; say simply “they were not free.” Children from harsh homes may hear rules as punishment, so stress that the rules protect.',
'Ask the children for one way to love God (pray, go to Mass, say his name with respect). Ask “Did God give rules before or after he rescued them?” Answer: after.',
]),
'1-3-1': dict(teach=[
'The one thing: Jesus is God’s own Son who became a real child and lived in a family, so he understands us. At six this makes God close and human, not just far away.',
'CCC 422: God sent his Son, born of a woman, to redeem us. CCC 456: for us and for our salvation he came down from heaven and was made man. CCC 531: Jesus lived an ordinary hidden life at Nazareth, obeying his parents and working.',
'Say: “Jesus is God and he became a baby, a boy, a helper at home, who learned and prayed.” To “Who is Jesus’ real dad?”, say: “God is his Father in heaven; Joseph looked after him like a dad on earth.”',
'Families differ: single parents, grandparents, foster carers, absent fathers. Praise the Holy Family as one family among many and avoid implying an ideal makes others less. Do not call Jesus a pretend man or God in disguise.',
'Ask: “What did Jesus do at home in Nazareth?” and “Who looks after you?” Listen for Jesus helping, praying, obeying. If a child says he did not need to learn, retell Luke 2:52: he grew in wisdom.',
]),
'1-3-2': dict(teach=[
'Get across that Jesus shows God’s love by what he says and does: he teaches, heals, and calls friends to help him. It matters because children learn who God is by seeing Jesus acting.',
'CCC 542: Jesus gathers people into God’s family by his word and signs. CCC 547: his miracles show the kingdom is present and that he is the Messiah. CCC 551: he chose twelve apostles to share his mission and authority.',
'Say: “Jesus healed the man with his friends’ help, to show God cares for our bodies and our hearts.” To “Why doesn’t Jesus heal everyone now?”, say: “He is always with the sick, and he asks us to care for them; one day there will be no more sickness.”',
'Children who are ill or have a sick relative may feel left out. Never imply illness means lack of faith. Mark 2 also forgives sin, which is too deep for this age; focus on friends bringing the man to Jesus.',
'Ask: “Who brought the man to Jesus?” and “Can you name an apostle?” A child who says Jesus loves and helps people, and picks a friend’s name for Peter or Andrew, has got it.',
]),
'1-3-3': dict(teach=[
'The core message: Jesus gave his life out of love for us, and God raised him on the third day, so Jesus is alive. Make Easter joy the final note; Good Friday is sad but never hopeless.',
'CCC 571: the cross and Resurrection are the center of the Good News. CCC 638: the Resurrection is the crowning truth of our faith. CCC 654: by death Christ frees us from sin; by rising he opens new life.',
'Say: “Jesus died because he loves us and wanted to take away our sin. Three days later God raised him. He is alive and we will be with him.” To “Why did he die?”, say: “To show love so big that even death could not stop it.”',
'Death is real for some of the children (a pet, grandparent). Name sadness, and say Jesus rose and promised life. No gore, no blame on any people; do not say that God needed to hurt his Son. Avoid saying Jesus was asleep.',
'Ask: “Why was Jesus on the cross?” and “What happened on Sunday?” Listen for love and “he came alive.” Then ask for one Easter sign (empty tomb, candle, Alleluia).',
]),
'1-4-1': dict(teach=[
'Get across that the Holy Spirit is God with us and in us, and Jesus sent him as our helper. The point at six is that we are never alone and can ask for help to love and be brave.',
'CCC 691: Holy Spirit is the proper name of the one we adore with the Father and the Son. CCC 731–732: on Pentecost the Spirit was poured out as a divine person and the Trinity was fully revealed.',
'Say: “The Holy Spirit is God, who is with us like a helper inside our hearts, giving us love, courage and the right words.” To “Where is the Spirit?”, say: “You cannot see him, as you cannot see wind, but you see what he does.”',
'The word “ghost” in older prayers, or ghost stories, may frighten children, so say Spirit and explain. Do not describe him only as wind or flame, or as a force; he is a Person. Wind and fire are signs.',
'Ask: “How does the Holy Spirit help us?” Listen for love, pray, brave. Then ask whether he is a person or a thing; the answer should be God, one of the three Persons.',
]),
'1-4-2': dict(teach=[
'The point: the Church is God’s family, and through Baptism each of us belongs, with leaders who care for us. A child who feels at home in the parish learns that faith is shared, not just private.',
'CCC 751: Church means a people called together by God. CCC 771: Christ established and sustains the Church as a visible community. CCC 881: Jesus made Peter the rock and gave him the keys; the Pope succeeds him as shepherd.',
'Say: “The Church is not just a building. It is all God’s people, like a big family, with the Pope as a shepherd for everyone and the bishop and priest near us.” To “Is the Pope God?”, say: “No, he is a man who serves Jesus’ family.”',
'Parish hurt, divorce, or leaving the Church can be a family sorrow; do not suggest that those outside do not belong to God’s love. Avoid describing the Church as only rules and leaders; speak of the pastor by name warmly.',
'Show photos of the Pope, bishop and pastor and ask who each is. Then ask: “Who is in God’s family?” An answer of “everyone who is baptized, including me” shows it landed.',
]),
'1-4-3': dict(teach=[
'Get across just this: there is one God in three Persons, Father, Son and Holy Spirit, and we are baptized and bless ourselves in that name. Children need not understand it, only know it is a mystery of love.',
'CCC 232: we are baptized in the name of the Father, the Son and the Holy Spirit. CCC 253: the Trinity is one; we do not confess three Gods but one God in three Persons, each whole and entire God.',
'Say: “God is a family of love: Father, Son and Spirit, three Persons, one God. We make the Sign of the Cross to say it.” To “How can 3 be 1?”, say: “It is a mystery, bigger than we can fit in our heads, and God told us about it himself.”',
'Avoid analogies that mislead: three parts like a shamrock or an egg suggest God is divided, and water in three forms suggests modes. Admit it is a mystery and give the child the words and the sign.',
'Ask the children to make the Sign of the Cross slowly and say the names. Ask “How many Gods?” Answer one. A child who adds that there are three Persons is on track.',
]),
'1-5-1': dict(teach=[
'The aim: Mary is Jesus’ mother and also ours, and she always points us to him. Children who see Mary as a loving mother find her easy to love without confusing her with God.',
'CCC 495: Mary is truly the Mother of God, the mother of Jesus. CCC 501: her spiritual motherhood extends to all people Jesus came to save. CCC 963: she is mother of the Church. Honor to her leads to Christ.',
'Say: “Mary said yes to God, and Jesus gave her to us as our mother at the cross. She loves us and prays for us.” To “Is Mary God?”, say: “No, she is Jesus’ human mother, the greatest of God’s creatures, and she loves us.”',
'Some children have lost a mother or have a hard relationship with one. Present Mary as a gift, not a replacement or a requirement. Avoid saying the Hail Mary makes Mary more important than Jesus.',
'Ask: “What did Mary say to God?” Listen for yes. Then say the first half of the Hail Mary together and ask who Mary leads us to.',
]),
'1-5-2': dict(teach=[
'The message: saints are ordinary people who loved Jesus, now with him in heaven, and they pray for us and show us how. Holiness at six is within reach: kind, brave, honest.',
'CCC 828: canonization recognizes people who lived heroic virtue by God’s grace. CCC 957: those in heaven stay united with us and intercede for us; we honor them as examples and ask their prayers.',
'Say: “Saints are God’s friends who are in heaven. We ask them to pray for us like we ask a friend.” To “Did saints ever do wrong?”, say: “Yes, they said sorry and kept trying with God’s help.”',
'Avoid presenting saints as perfect, scary or only miracle workers; skip gruesome martyrdom details. Do not say we worship saints; we honor them and worship God alone. Pick child-friendly saints.',
'Ask each child to name a saint and one thing the saint did to love Jesus. Then ask “Who can you copy this week?” A concrete small act shows it landed.',
]),
'1-5-3': dict(teach=[
'Key message: God gave each of us an angel to watch over us and help us towards heaven. The comfort of not being alone helps children at night and in worry, as long as it is tied to God’s care.',
'CCC 332: angels serve God’s plan throughout history. CCC 336: from infancy to death human life is surrounded by their watchful care and intercession; beside each believer stands an angel as protector.',
'Say: “Angels are God’s messengers who don’t have bodies, and each of us has one to help us do right and be safe.” To “Can I see my angel?”, say: “Usually not, but God’s angel is near you and loves you.”',
'Do not use angels to scare children into good behavior, and do not blame the angel when something bad happens. Angels are not dead people, or chubby babies; pictures are symbols. Avoid fear of the dark becoming reliance on charms.',
'Teach the Guardian Angel prayer and ask a child to say what an angel does. Listen for helps, protects, leads to God and not magic. Ask who sends the angel: God.',
]),
'1-6-1': dict(teach=[
'The aim: in Baptism God makes us his children, forgives sin and puts us in the Church. Children should hear “I belong to God and to his family because he loved me first.”',
'CCC 1213: Baptism is the gateway to life in the Spirit and frees us from sin, and we are reborn as sons of God. CCC 1239: it joins us to Christ and the Trinity. CCC 1262: water, oil, garment and candle signify its effects.',
'Say: “The water washes us clean, the oil strengthens us, the white garment shows new life and the candle says Jesus is our light.” To “Why was I baptized as a baby?”, say: “Your parents wanted God’s gift for you.”',
'Some children are unbaptized, have different traditions or have never been told. Welcome them warmly; never imply they are loved less. Be careful not to treat the water as magic or saying baptism makes children automatically good.',
'Pass around sign pictures (water, oil, garment, candle) and ask what each means. Ask “What does Baptism make us?” Answer: God’s children and members of the Church.',
]),
'1-6-2': dict(teach=[
'The one thing: at Mass we meet Jesus, who speaks in his word and gives himself in Holy Communion. At six the goal is to feel at home and know what happens, not to follow every detail.',
'CCC 1346: the Mass has two parts, the Liturgy of the Word and the Liturgy of the Eucharist. CCC 1348: all gather, with Christ himself as principal priest. Emmaus shows Jesus known in the Word and the breaking of bread.',
'Say: “At Mass we listen to God, say thank you and receive Jesus. It is like a family meal with Jesus.” To “Is it really Jesus?”, say: “Yes, Catholics believe that after the priest’s prayer the bread and wine become Jesus, though they still look the same.”',
'Children not yet receiving Communion may feel left out; name a blessing or spiritual communion. Avoid calling Mass boring or presenting it only as an obligation. Be gentle with restless children.',
'Lay out Mass picture cards out of order and ask children to put them in order. Ask: “Whom do we meet at Mass?” Listen for Jesus. Practice one response together.',
]),
'1-6-3': dict(teach=[
'Teach that God is always ready to forgive, and Reconciliation lets us say sorry and hear his forgiveness through the priest. The feeling to aim for is relief and trust, never dread.',
'CCC 1440: sin damages our friendship with God and the Church, and conversion brings forgiveness and reconciliation. CCC 1441: only God forgives sin; Jesus gives that power. CCC 1449: the priest absolves in the name of the Father, Son and Spirit.',
'Say: “When we do wrong we tell God we are sorry, and he is glad to forgive, like the shepherd who finds his lost sheep. In Reconciliation the priest speaks for Jesus.” To “Will the priest tell?”, say: “No, never; it is a secret.”',
'Never use the sacrament as a threat or punishment, and watch for scrupulous or anxious children. Do not expect lists of sins; a few simple ones suffice. Some children’s “sins” are accidents; teach the difference gently.',
'Ask: “What does God do when we say sorry?” Listen for forgives and is glad. Also ask what words we say; a child who offers “I am sorry” and “I will try again” has understood.',
]),
'1-7-1': dict(teach=[
'Get across that we love as Jesus teaches by treating others the way we want to be treated. At six this turns faith into small daily choices on the playground and at home.',
'CCC 1789 names the Golden Rule (Matthew 7:12) as a rule that applies in every case. CCC 2055: Jesus sums up the Law as love of God and neighbor. We do it because we love God, not only to be fair.',
'Say: “How do you want people to treat you? Do that to them.” To “What if they are mean to me?”, say: “We still try to be kind, and we tell a grown-up if we are hurt or unsafe. Being kind does not mean letting someone hurt you.”',
'Do not suggest children should stay quiet when bullied or hurt. Avoid reducing the rule to “be nice so they like you.” Notice children who are often left out and be careful role-playing unkindness.',
'Give a short scene (someone has no one to play with) and ask what a friend of Jesus does. Listen for specific actions. Later watch for the rule in play.',
]),
'1-7-2': dict(teach=[
'The aim: honoring parents and teachers means listening and respecting them because they care for us, as Jesus did at Nazareth. At six this is about trust and safety, not blind fear.',
'CCC 2214: the honor owed to parents is rooted in God’s fatherhood. CCC 2216: respect shows in docility and obedience. This is the fourth commandment, which promises blessing and belongs with love of God.',
'Say: “Grown-ups help us stay safe and learn, so we listen and say thank you. Jesus obeyed Mary and Joseph.” To “What if they ask something wrong?”, say: “God comes first; tell another grown-up you trust.”',
'Family situations vary: some children live with an unsafe or absent parent or with foster carers. Say “the grown-ups who care for you.” Never suggest obeying anyone who hurts them; teach telling a safe adult.',
'Ask: “Who takes care of you, and how can you show respect?” Listen for concrete ways like listening, helping, thanking. Praise honest answers about finding it hard.',
]),
'1-7-3': dict(teach=[
'Teach two linked ideas: the world is God’s gift to look after, and Jesus asks us to share with the poor. Children can do real, small acts of care and sharing.',
'CCC 2415: the seventh commandment calls for respect for creation. CCC 2443: God blesses those who help the poor, and Matthew 25 shows that what we do for the least we do for Christ. Genesis 2:15 gives humans the garden to tend.',
'Say: “God lets us look after his world, so we don’t waste or litter, and we share what we have with people in need, because Jesus is in them.” To “Why are people poor?”, say: “Sometimes things are unfair, and God asks us all to help.”',
'Some children in the class may be the ones in need, so avoid “poor people” as a distant group; speak of “families who need help.” Do not shame what children have. Give a task they can complete and keep it dignified.',
'Choose one action (collect for a food bank, tidy the yard, plant a seed) and ask who it helps and why. Listen for “Jesus is in the people we help” or “God gave us the world.”',
]),
'1-8-1': dict(teach=[
'The point: prayer is talking and listening to God, a friend who loves us, and it comes in four main ways: praise, thanks, asking and sorry. Children can start straight away in their own words.',
'CCC 2626: blessing and adoration are a response to God’s gifts. CCC 2629–2630: petition is the most spontaneous form, and the Church’s prayer is buoyed by hope. CCC 2623: the Spirit teaches us to pray. Say these as simple prayer types.',
'Say: “Prayer is talking to God like a good friend: wow, thank you, please, and sorry.” To “Does God hear me?”, say: “Yes, always. Sometimes the answer is wait or no, because he knows what is best, but he listens.”',
'Avoid treating prayer as a way to get things, or promising that God will give whatever we ask. A child who prayed for a sick parent or pet who died may feel God did not listen; acknowledge it and say God is with them.',
'Let the children say one prayer of each type aloud or by gesture (hands up, hands together, open hands). Ask which type they prayed. Their own words show it landed.',
]),
'1-8-2': dict(teach=[
'The aim: the Our Father is the prayer Jesus himself gave, and the Hail Mary is how we pray with Mary. Children should learn the words and know that they are talking to our loving Father.',
'CCC 2759: the Lord gave his disciples the Our Father in answer to their request. CCC 2676: the Hail Mary joins the angel Gabriel’s greeting and Elizabeth’s words with a request for Mary’s prayers.',
'Say: “Jesus taught us to say ‘Our Father,’ which means God is our dad. In the Hail Mary we ask Mary to pray for us.” To “Why do we say ‘hallowed’?”, say: “It means holy, so we say God’s name is very special.”',
'Children who lost a father or have a difficult one may struggle with Father; stress that God is a perfect, loving Father. Avoid rote speed; check that words like trespass or womb are explained or skipped.',
'Pray the Our Father slowly with actions, then ask a child to explain one line in their words. Invite children to say the first half of the Hail Mary alone or in pairs.',
]),
'1-8-3': dict(teach=[
'Get across that the Church year helps us live Jesus’ story through the year, and each season has its own color and sign. Children love signs and pattern, and the year gives faith a rhythm.',
'CCC 1163: the Church celebrates Christ’s work through the year, beginning with Sunday. CCC 1168–1169: Easter is the source and the Feast of feasts, with the whole liturgical year around it. Colors follow tradition.',
'Say: “Advent we wait, Christmas we celebrate Jesus’ birth, Lent we prepare, and Easter we celebrate that he is alive.” To “Why is Lent sad?”, say: “It is not sad, it is a time to get ready, pray and share so Easter joy is bigger.”',
'Lent and Advent can become sweet-giving or rules for some families, and Christmas can be hard for children without a home or family. Avoid making seasons only about gifts or sacrifices. Note different customs at home.',
'Show four seasonal signs and ask children to match each with its season. Ask which season we are in now. Listen for the right sign and for Jesus, not only gifts or eggs.',
]),
'1-L1': dict(teach=[
'The goal is that telling the truth feels safe because God and the people who love them are glad. At six lies are about fear of trouble or embarrassment, so show that truth is less scary than hiding.',
'CCC 2464: the eighth commandment forbids misrepresenting the truth, because God is truth. CCC 2482: a lie is speaking a falsehood with the intent to deceive. CCC 1776: conscience is the voice in our heart calling us to do good.',
'Say: “Your conscience is a little voice in your heart that says ‘tell the truth.’ If you did something wrong, say it, say sorry, and fix it.” To “What if I get in trouble?”, say: “You may, but God and we are proud that you were honest, and it feels lighter.”',
'Do not punish honesty; if a child admits something, thank them first. Do not call a child a liar. Pretend play, imagination and mistakes are not lies. Safe grown-ups matter: some children fear harsh reactions at home.',
'Ask: “What can you say when you did something wrong?” Listen for “I did it, I am sorry.” Later, notice and praise a truthful moment with a specific comment.',
]),
'1-L2': dict(teach=[
'Teach one habit: look around, notice who is alone, and invite them in. Six-year-olds know the sting of being left out; making it an action gives them a way to love like Jesus.',
'CCC 1700: human dignity is rooted in being made in God’s image. CCC 1822: charity is love of God and neighbor for God’s sake. CCC 2447: works of mercy include comforting. Matthew 25:40 says what we do to the least we do to Jesus.',
'Say: “Everyone is made by God and is special. If someone is alone, you can say ‘Want to play?’ That is how we love Jesus.” To “What if my friends say no?”, say: “Try a small thing, like saying hi, and ask a teacher for help.”',
'Some children are the ones left out, so avoid language that shames them as a project. Do not push children to befriend someone unsafe; tell an adult about bullying. Friendships cannot be forced, but kindness can be asked.',
'Ask: “What can you do if someone is alone at recess?” Listen for a concrete invitation. Later ask if anyone invited someone and how they felt.',
]),
'1-L3': dict(teach=[
'The message: the world and our bodies are God’s good gifts, so we thank him by taking care of them. At six this means small habits and wonder, not worry about the planet.',
'CCC 339: each creature has its own goodness, and God saw that it was good. CCC 2415–2418: we respect creation and owe animals kindness, though they are not equal to people. CCC 2288: life and health are precious gifts to care for.',
'Say: “God made everything good, including you. We say thank you by caring for plants and animals, washing hands, resting and eating well.” To “Why do animals die?”, say: “All living things have a time, and God cares for them; we are gentle with them.”',
'Avoid heavy climate fear, body shame, or food moralizing; some children have illness, disability, or little choice about food or sleep. Keep it positive, and tell a grown-up when sick or hurt. Do not equate animals with people.',
'Ask for one way to care for the world and one for their body. Listen for specific choices and a thank-you to God. Check a child’s plant or hand washing the following week.',
]),
}
